;; Kanata 1.12.1-prerelease-1 or newer; see the F+J note below for why.
;; Framework Laptop US-ANSI QWERTY with mirrored home-row modifiers:
;;   A S D F = left  Meta Alt Ctrl Shift
;;   J K L ; = right Shift Ctrl Alt  Meta
;;
;; Home-row modifiers are for shortcuts only. Dedicated Shift types capitals,
;; so the home-row Shift never has to win a race against ordinary typing and is
;; tuned as defensively as every other home-row key.
;;
;; Typing always wins. A home-row key only becomes a modifier when nothing about
;; the keystroke looks like typing: no key pressed just before it, and either an
;; opposite-hand key completed on top of it or a deliberate hold past the
;; timeout. Everything else types the letter.
;;
;; One rule decides every home-row key, and it is prior idle. A home-row key
;; pressed while typing is in progress is its letter, full stop. Only a key that
;; starts a burst is a modifier candidate at all, and even then it becomes a
;; modifier only if an opposite-hand key completes on top of it or it is held
;; past the timeout. No home-row key is paired to any other: F+J below is the
;; single exception, and it is bounded by a timing window rather than by which
;; keys happen to follow which.
;;
;; However many modifiers a shortcut wants, exactly one tap-hold decision is
;; made. The first home-row key of the group decides; the moment it resolves to
;; a modifier it switches its own hand to the lmods/rmods layer, where every
;; remaining home-row key is a plain modifier with no decision left to make.
;; Each modifier re-asserts the layer while it is held, so the layer outlives
;; whichever key opened it and modifiers can be released one at a time: open
;; with D, add Shift on F, release D, and Shift is left holding the layer.
;;
;; There are two ways in:
;;
;;   - Roll onto the opposite hand: K then A is Ctrl+A, resolved when A is
;;     released. Add further modifiers from the K hand once inside the layer.
;;   - Press a group on one hand and hold it past the timeout. Same-hand keys
;;     landing on an undecided one are queued rather than settling it, and they
;;     replay into the layer the first key opens, so J K L held together is
;;     Shift+Ctrl+Alt and A S D F is all four. Neither the order they were
;;     pressed in nor the spacing between them matters. Once the layer is open,
;;     any key on either hand is the target, same hand included -- which is what
;;     Ctrl+click, Ctrl+Space and Ctrl+Shift need.
;;
;; F+J is Escape, as a chord, which is the one form of it whose exposure is
;; bounded by a timing window rather than open-ended. The earlier attempt at it
;; through hold-on-press had no window at all -- F followed by J was Escape at
;; any spacing -- which is what made it eat input.
;;
;; The window is the only guard, deliberately. There is no chords-v2-min-idle:
;; leaving Escape closed for a while after the last keystroke would break the
;; case it exists for, which is leaving vim insert mode the instant a word is
;; finished. So the chord stays armed mid-word, and the timing carries it: two
;; keys landing within combo-timeout of each other is a gesture, not typing.
;;
;; The window used to cost more than the window. Chords-v2 held its participants
;; back from the prior-idle check, so a shortcut starting on F or J was eaten
;; when its target followed inside combo-timeout, and F- or J-led modifier
;; groups did not work at all. jtroo/kanata#2117 fixed that, and it is the
;; reason this config wants 1.12.1-prerelease-1: on 1.12.0 those two keys still
;; pay, and nothing else does.
;;
;; Meta is on A and G, mirrored on ; and H, so both a pinky and an index-finger
;; Meta are available to compare. Holding one never sends a lone Super to the
;; desktop; see hold-lmet below.

(defcfg
  process-unmapped-keys yes
  concurrent-tap-hold yes

  ;; The primary defense. A home-row key pressed within this window of any other
  ;; key is its letter, immediately, with no waiting state and no decision to
  ;; get wrong. This covers every home-row key inside a burst of typing; only a
  ;; key that starts a burst is ever a modifier candidate at all.
  tap-hold-require-prior-idle 200
)

;; Caps Lock and Escape are swapped here rather than through an xkb option.
;; Doing it downstream of Kanata swapped Kanata's own Escape output back into
;; Caps Lock, which is what the F+J chord below was really emitting.
;;
;; Physical handedness. The hardware Fn key is handled by the keyboard firmware
;; and does not emit a normal keycode, so it is not part of this map. Tab, Enter,
;; Backspace, Escape, Caps Lock, and Delete are placed on the hand that actually
;; reaches them so that Shift+Tab, Ctrl+Backspace and friends resolve as holds.
;; Space is deliberately unassigned: it is the one key common enough right after
;; a home-row letter that treating it as a hold trigger would misfire. Dedicated
;; modifiers are excluded via hrm-neutral below rather than by handedness.
(defhands
  (left
    esc f1 f2 f3 f4 f5 f6
    grv 1 2 3 4 5
    tab q w e r t
    caps a s d f g
    z x c v b)

  (right
    f7 f8 f9 f10 f11 f12 del
    6 7 8 9 0 - = bspc
    y u i o p [ ] \
    h j k l ; ' ret
    n m , . /
    up left down rght))

(defvar
  ;; Hold this long to enter the modifier layer. It is also the dwell limit on
  ;; the first letter of a word that starts on the home row: rest on the S of
  ;; "sad" longer than this and it is Alt instead. Raising it buys dwell
  ;; tolerance and costs exactly as much extra holding to reach a shortcut,
  ;; which lands about 40ms after this expires.
  hrm-hold 300

  ;; Auto-repeat is reached by tapping a home-row key and then pressing it again
  ;; within this window, which holds the letter down instead of starting another
  ;; modifier decision. Measured from the release, that leaves about 100ms for
  ;; the second press. The window is the same key's dead zone: while it is open
  ;; that key cannot start a shortcut, so a tap of D followed by reaching for
  ;; Ctrl repeats "d" instead. 500 was tried, and buying a comfortable double-tap
  ;; costs half a second of that key each time it is tapped. Typing is unaffected
  ;; at any value -- a replayed letter does not arm the window, so doubles like
  ;; "add" and vim's dd are untouched.
  tap-repress 150

  ;; The F+J window, and the only thing guarding it. Escape fires when both keys
  ;; land within this long; a shortcut starting on F or J is eaten if its target
  ;; follows within it; and an "fj" typed this fast becomes Escape. All three are
  ;; the same window, so this number is the whole tradeoff: wide enough for a
  ;; deliberate two-finger press (10-30ms), under the gap between pressing a
  ;; modifier and reaching its target, and under any real typing roll -- at 45ms
  ;; spacing every sequence of home-row keys types cleanly, "fjord" included.
  combo-timeout 35

  ;; Dedicated modifier keys must not settle a pending home-row modifier;
  ;; they are meant to stack with one. Reused by every HRM alias below.
  hrm-neutral (neutral-keys lsft rsft lctl rctl lmet lalt ralt)
)

;; A lone Super press-and-release opens the desktop launcher, which a home-row
;; Meta would trigger every time it was held and then released without a target.
;; f24 is kanata's conventional placeholder: a real keycode nothing binds. The
;; hold presses it alongside Meta, so the desktop sees a non-modifier key during
;; the hold and the release is never lone. It is let go 5ms later rather than
;; held, so it cannot auto-repeat underneath the modifier.
;;
;; Alt has the same lone-press behaviour in GTK and Qt, where it focuses the
;; menu bar. Add f24 and the release macro to hr-s and hr-l to cover that too.
(defvirtualkeys
  rel-f24 (release-key f24))

(defalias
  hold-lmet (multi lmet f24 (layer-while-held lmods) (macro 5 (on-press tap-vkey rel-f24)))
  hold-rmet (multi rmet f24 (layer-while-held rmods) (macro 5 (on-press tap-vkey rel-f24)))

  ;; Every modifier re-asserts its own layer while held, so the layer outlives
  ;; the key that opened it and survives releasing modifiers one at a time.
  ml-alt (multi lalt (layer-while-held lmods))
  ml-ctl (multi lctl (layer-while-held lmods))
  ml-sft (multi lsft (layer-while-held lmods))
  mr-alt (multi ralt (layer-while-held rmods))
  mr-ctl (multi rctl (layer-while-held rmods))
  mr-sft (multi rsft (layer-while-held rmods))

  ;; tap-hold-opposite-hand-release waits for the opposite-hand target key to be
  ;; pressed AND released before committing to the modifier. This is what makes
  ;; the config safe for a rolling typist: in a roll the home-row key comes up
  ;; while the next key is still down, and a home-row key released first is
  ;; always its letter. Only dwelling on the home-row key past the other key's
  ;; release produces a modifier.
  ;;
  ;; same-hand ignore is what turns a group of home-row keys on one hand into a
  ;; group of modifiers. A same-hand press neither settles the pending decision
  ;; nor is thrown away: it waits, and replays once the first key resolves. The
  ;; cost lands in one place only, the first letter of a word that starts on the
  ;; home row. That letter now emits when its key is released rather than when
  ;; the next key is pressed, roughly 60ms later at typing speed, and dwelling
  ;; on it past hrm-hold produces a modifier. Nothing inside a burst is touched:
  ;; prior idle has already pinned those keys to letters before this is asked.
  ;;
  ;; neutral ignore lets a dedicated modifier stack with a pending HRM instead
  ;; of collapsing it to a tap. unknown-hand tap keeps Space tap-biased; reach
  ;; Ctrl+Space through the hold-then-layer path. timeout hold is the layer
  ;; entry: a deliberate hold with nothing else pressed is a modifier. Hold a
  ;; home-row key to repeat its letter by tapping it first, which tap-repress
  ;; turns into an ordinary auto-repeat.

  hr-a (tap-hold-opposite-hand-release $hrm-hold a @hold-lmet
         (same-hand ignore)
         (unknown-hand tap)
         $hrm-neutral
         (neutral ignore)
         (timeout hold)
         (tap-repress-timeout $tap-repress))

  hr-s (tap-hold-opposite-hand-release $hrm-hold s @ml-alt
         (same-hand ignore)
         (unknown-hand tap)
         $hrm-neutral
         (neutral ignore)
         (timeout hold)
         (tap-repress-timeout $tap-repress))

  hr-d (tap-hold-opposite-hand-release $hrm-hold d @ml-ctl
         (same-hand ignore)
         (unknown-hand tap)
         $hrm-neutral
         (neutral ignore)
         (timeout hold)
         (tap-repress-timeout $tap-repress))

  hr-f (tap-hold-opposite-hand-release $hrm-hold f @ml-sft
         (same-hand ignore)
         (unknown-hand tap)
         $hrm-neutral
         (neutral ignore)
         (timeout hold)
         (tap-repress-timeout $tap-repress))

  hr-g (tap-hold-opposite-hand-release $hrm-hold g @hold-lmet
         (same-hand ignore)
         (unknown-hand tap)
         $hrm-neutral
         (neutral ignore)
         (timeout hold)
         (tap-repress-timeout $tap-repress))

  hr-h (tap-hold-opposite-hand-release $hrm-hold h @hold-rmet
         (same-hand ignore)
         (unknown-hand tap)
         $hrm-neutral
         (neutral ignore)
         (timeout hold)
         (tap-repress-timeout $tap-repress))

  hr-j (tap-hold-opposite-hand-release $hrm-hold j @mr-sft
         (same-hand ignore)
         (unknown-hand tap)
         $hrm-neutral
         (neutral ignore)
         (timeout hold)
         (tap-repress-timeout $tap-repress))

  hr-k (tap-hold-opposite-hand-release $hrm-hold k @mr-ctl
         (same-hand ignore)
         (unknown-hand tap)
         $hrm-neutral
         (neutral ignore)
         (timeout hold)
         (tap-repress-timeout $tap-repress))

  hr-l (tap-hold-opposite-hand-release $hrm-hold l @mr-alt
         (same-hand ignore)
         (unknown-hand tap)
         $hrm-neutral
         (neutral ignore)
         (timeout hold)
         (tap-repress-timeout $tap-repress))

  hr-; (tap-hold-opposite-hand-release $hrm-hold ; @hold-rmet
         (same-hand ignore)
         (unknown-hand tap)
         $hrm-neutral
         (neutral ignore)
         (timeout hold)
         (tap-repress-timeout $tap-repress))

)

;; first-release so Escape repeats no further than one keypress, and disabled on
;; the modifier layers, where F and J are already modifiers.
(defchordsv2
  (f j) esc $combo-timeout first-release (lmods rmods))

;; Framework Laptop US keyboard. The Fn key is not visible to Kanata.
(defsrc
  esc  f1   f2   f3   f4   f5   f6   f7   f8   f9   f10  f11  f12  del

  grv  1    2    3    4    5    6    7    8    9    0    -    =    bspc

  tab  q    w    e    r    t    y    u    i    o    p    [    ]    \

  caps a    s    d    f    g    h    j    k    l    ;    '    ret

  lsft z    x    c    v    b    n    m    ,    .    /    rsft

  lctl lmet lalt            spc            ralt rctl
                                      left up down rght
)

;; G and H are a second Meta alongside A and ;, kept so both positions can be
;; compared in use. They are the frequency risk in this layer: H especially is
;; common enough that if stray Metas show up while typing, these two are the
;; first thing to drop back to plain letters. They stay Meta in the layers below
;; either way, so dropping them here costs no shortcut.
(deflayer base
  caps f1    f2    f3    f4    f5   f6   f7    f8    f9    f10   f11   f12  del

  grv  1     2     3     4     5    6    7     8     9     0     -     =     bspc

  tab  q     w     e     r     t    y    u     i     o     p     [     ]     \

  esc  @hr-a @hr-s @hr-d @hr-f @hr-g @hr-h @hr-j @hr-k @hr-l @hr-; '     ret

  lsft z     x     c     v     b    n    m     ,     .     /     rsft

  lctl lmet  lalt              spc              ralt rctl
                                          left up down rght
)

;; Once a home-row modifier on one hand has resolved, that whole hand becomes
;; plain modifiers: no tap-hold decision, no timing, no way to get it wrong.
;; The opposite hand's home row is pinned back to plain letters at the same
;; time, so a target key there is instant and repeats normally instead of
;; running its own tap-hold decision underneath a held modifier.
;;
;; That pinning is why all four modifiers live on each hand: a shortcut takes
;; every modifier from one hand and its target from anywhere, rather than
;; splitting modifiers across both. Every other key stays transparent, so
;; same-hand targets like Ctrl+C work once the layer is open.
;;
;; These must stay below the base layer: Kanata treats the first layer in the
;; file as the default one.
(deflayermap (lmods)
  a @hold-lmet  s @ml-alt  d @ml-ctl  f @ml-sft  g @hold-lmet
  h h           j j        k k        l l        ; ;)

(deflayermap (rmods)
  h @hold-rmet  j @mr-sft  k @mr-ctl  l @mr-alt  ; @hold-rmet
  a a           s s        d d        f f        g g)
